course: dictionary
lesson: 7611
title: Prisoner: a person who is kept
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A prisoner is a person who is kept somewhere and is not free to leave. Most often this means someone locked up in prison as a punishment for a crime. The word can also describe someone captured in war, or someone who feels trapped.
explain: Context tells you which meaning is meant. «In prison Joseph helped the other prisoners» shows people kept inside a prison. «A prisoner of war» tells you the person was caught during a war. «A prisoner of fear» is not a real cell at all: it means someone who feels unable to escape a feeling.
explain: Look for clues such as prison, castle, dungeon, war or trapped. The word «prisoners» is the plural, meaning more than one person. Try your meaning in the sentence: if «people kept against their will» fits, you have it.
fact: A prisoner is a person who is kept and is not allowed to leave.
fact: «Prisoner» can mean someone locked up for a crime, someone captured in war, or someone who feels trapped.
fact: «Prisoner of war» means someone caught and held during a war.
fact: Elizabeth Fry began visiting women prisoners in Newgate prison in London in 1813, and she taught them to read and sew.
fact: The plural of «prisoner» is «prisoners».
exercise: «In prison Joseph helped the other prisoners.» What does prisoners mean here? | Visitors who bring food | *People kept in prison | People who guard the prison | Children who go to school
exercise: «Elizabeth Fry visited women prisoners in Newgate prison.» Which clue shows the women were kept inside? | *Newgate prison | Visited | Taught | Women
exercise: «She felt like a prisoner of fear, too scared to step outside.» What does prisoner of fear mean? | Someone who is paid to guard fear | A person who ran away from home | *Someone trapped by fear | A person who likes being scared
test: A prisoner of war is best described as | Someone who won a war | A person who writes history books | *Someone caught and held during a war | A soldier who is on holiday
test: What is the plural of prisoner? | *prisoners | prisonery | prisoner's | prison
exam: «Badger says march the prisoners upstairs and make them clean the bedrooms.» What tells you the prisoners cannot choose what they do? | The bedrooms need clean sheets | The soap is fresh | *They are marched and made to work | Mole is trusted with the job
